{
  "id": "remove-nth-node-from-end-of-list",
  "questionId": "remove-nth-node-from-end-of-list",
  "slug": "remove-nth-node-from-end-of-list",
  "title": "Remove Nth Node From End of List",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Fast slow pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "fast & slow pointers",
    "linked list",
    "two pointers"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75",
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "you could count the length first and compute the index directly - can you find the same node in a single pass instead?",
    "what if you started one pointer n steps ahead of another, then moved both forward at the same speed?",
    "advance a 'fast' pointer n steps from the head first, then start moving both fast and a 'slow' pointer one step at a time together",
    "when fast reaches the end of the list, slow is right before the node that needs to be removed - unlink it (or, in array terms, skip that index when building the result)"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "off-by-one when advancing the lead pointer n steps, ending up pointing at the wrong node",
    "not handling the case where the node to remove is the head (n equals the list length)",
    "forgetting that removing the only node should return an empty list"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A singly linked list is given to you as an array `nums`, where `nums[i]`\nis the value of the i-th node in order, along with an integer `n`.\n\n**Remove the n-th node counting from the end of the list (n = 1 means the last node) and return the resulting list's values in order.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 30",
      "-100 <= nums[i] <= 100",
      "1 <= n <= nums.length"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "removing the head (n equals the list length)",
      "removing the tail (n = 1)",
      "a single-node list"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], n = 2",
        "output": "[1, 2, 3, 5]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 2, 3, 5]",
        "explanation": "the 2nd node from the end is 4, removing it leaves [1,2,3,5]"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1], n = 1",
        "output": "[]",
        "expectedOutput": "[]",
        "explanation": "removing the only node leaves an empty list"
      },
      {
